# Formant Boogie

**Ingredients:** SUNDANESE KARINDING + BOOGIE / POST-DISCO + ANALOG BASS SYNTH + CLAVINET + DRUM MACHINE + TENOR SAXOPHONE + HAND PERCUSSION

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a dry-season residency in a former language-laboratory building in Bandung, where karinding player Raka Permana was recording isolated jaw-harp articulations through an old row of speech-analysis microphones while Jakarta producer Nadine Yusuf rebuilt early-1980s boogie grooves from drum machine, bass synth, clavinet, and dry tenor saxophone. Their first arrangement simply doubled the karinding with synth bass and treated its buzzing tone as a novelty. The useful discovery came when Nadine noticed that Raka could radically reshape the apparent pitch color without changing the vibrating tongue itself: opening the mouth, narrowing the oral cavity, changing tongue position, and shifting breath pressure produced moving formant peaks above a nearly constant fundamental. Rather than filtering a synth to imitate him, she inverted the relationship. The karinding became the source of the arrangement's spectral motion, and the electronic instruments were required to follow the mouth-shaped changes instead of generating their own sweeps. The result became a dance style where human resonance replaces conventional filter automation.

Formant Boogie is a dry, elastic acoustic-electronic funk style built from Sundanese karinding articulation and early-1980s boogie, post-disco, and synth-funk. It normally sits between 108 and 118 BPM in 4/4. The rhythm section is compact and deliberate: short drum-machine kick, crisp snare or clap, closed sixteenth hats, hand percussion, rubbery analog bass synth, clavinet, sparse electric piano, and dry tenor saxophone. The karinding sits in the center of the upper-mid spectrum and is recorded closely enough that mouth-shape changes become part of the composition.

The core aesthetic is **one vibrating tongue, many apparent vowels**. The karinding's flexible tongue supplies a recurring physical pulse while the performer's mouth cavity acts as an acoustic filter. Rather than moving through conventional melodic lines, the player sculpts resonant bands that can resemble closed, open, narrow, nasal, hollow, or rounded vowel colors. Those spectral shapes become the band's main source of motion.

The karinding is recorded with one very close small-diaphragm condenser slightly off-axis from the mouth, one dynamic microphone 30–50 cm away for dry body, and one optional contact or miniature microphone on the instrument frame when available. Breath and mouth noise are kept controlled but not erased. The instrument should retain the difference between a hard tongue release, soft pulse, breath-assisted articulation, and purely resonant cavity change.

Players favor repeated-pulse cells, octave-like overtone emphasis, fifth-related resonance bands, narrow formant sweeps, short rhythmic interruptions, and gradual changes in mouth shape across otherwise static figures. Conventional diatonic melody is secondary. The instrument is most effective when the listener can hear the same buzzing source apparently change identity without the player changing to a different instrument or synth patch.

The primary compositional device is the **Vowel Relay**. A karinding phrase moves through two or more clearly contrasted mouth shapes, and each formant state authorizes a different ensemble response. A dark closed-mouth resonance may cue bass synth only; an open bright resonance may add clavinet; a narrow high formant may permit tenor sax or handclaps. The acoustic spectral shift therefore becomes an arrangement switch.

A second device, **Human Filter Hold**, forbids the synthesizers from using obvious automated filter sweeps while the karinding is active. Bass and keys must remain spectrally stable so the moving formants are generated by the performer rather than by automation. Filter movement may return only when the karinding drops out.

A third device, **Jaw Gate**, removes one electronic event immediately after a strong karinding tongue release. A clap, clavinet note, bass attack, or hat can disappear for one sixteenth or eighth note, making the physical acoustic pulse feel larger without additional gain.

A fourth device, **Formant Chase**, assigns tenor saxophone or clavinet a pitch-area response that follows the perceived overtone focus rather than the karinding's fundamental. If the mouth cavity emphasizes a higher resonance, saxophone may answer in that register even though the jaw harp's fundamental remains unchanged. The ensemble therefore follows perceived color rather than literal note identity.

A fifth device, **Closed-Open Flip**, repeats the same rhythmic karinding pattern twice with opposite cavity shapes. The first pass is dark and narrow; the second is bright and open. Bass, drums, and harmony stay nearly identical. The section feels changed primarily because the acoustic filter state has flipped.

A sixth device, **Tongue Rest Bar**, inserts one full bar in which the karinding player stops plucking but keeps the instrument at the mouth. The band continues with the same groove while all formant motion disappears. When the jaw harp returns, the reappearance of mouth-shaped resonance functions like a new section without a conventional fill or riser.

Drums remain dry and compact. Kick is short and punchy. Snare is crisp with minimal room. Claps are narrow and controlled. Hi-hats use tight sixteenths with subtle velocity changes and occasional open accents. Percussion fills are short and linear. Disco-style four-on-the-floor is allowed in choruses, but verses often use slightly displaced kick patterns to preserve funk elasticity.

Hand percussion includes shaker, muted frame-drum taps, cowbell, or dry woodblock used selectively. Continuous busy conga patterns are avoided. Percussion exists to mark phrase edges and emphasize Vowel Relay changes rather than to create a separate folkloric layer.

Analog bass synth is rubbery and medium-short. Lines favor tonic, octave, fifth, flat seventh, sixth, and chromatic pickups. Portamento is rare. The bass should articulate groove while leaving enough midrange space for the karinding's resonant bands.

Clavinet supplies the principal rhythmic harmony through muted sixteenths, two-note stabs, dominant-seventh fragments, ninths, suspended tones, and occasional wah-like articulation performed manually rather than automated continuously. It may answer bright karinding formants but must not imitate them directly.

Electric piano is secondary and appears mainly in pre-choruses, bridges, and slower breakdowns. Voicings favor minor 7, major 7, dominant 9, 6/9, sus2, and add9 shapes. Chords are short enough to avoid masking the jaw harp.

Tenor saxophone supplies dry, human counterlines. It uses short falls, repeated notes, compact blues-derived cells, upper-register stabs, subtone lows, and two- or three-note answers. Extended jazz soloing is avoided. The saxophone is strongest when it responds to the perceived formant peak rather than to the written chord root.

Vocals are optional but encouraged. Sundanese, Indonesian, and English can coexist in original lyrics. Delivery should be close, rhythmic, and restrained: low-register verses, dry group replies, clipped spoken lines, and compact falsetto hooks. Lyrics work best around overheard speech, vowel sounds, cassette labels, rehearsal rooms, elevator mirrors, market shutters, late-night buses, and the strange fact that one unchanged object can sound different when the surrounding cavity changes.

Effects remain short and tactile. Karinding is mostly dry with compact room ambience. Bass synth receives mild saturation. Clavinet may use a short amp room or narrow phaser only in sections where the karinding is silent. Saxophone gets a small plate. Vocals may use brief slap delay. Avoid giant filter sweeps, EDM risers, trap hats, tropical-house plucks, cinematic Indonesian textures, fake jaw-harp samples, and heavy stereo widening on the karinding.

The defining tension is between electronic dance music's usual reliance on filter automation and a completely acoustic instrument whose spectral movement is produced by a living mouth cavity in real time.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 108–118 BPM, 4/4 boogie / post-disco with compact drum-machine kick, crisp snare or clap, tight sixteenth hats, occasional four-on-the-floor sections, rubbery bass synth, clavinet, and tightly placed karinding pulses.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dominant 7 and 9, minor 7, major 7, 6/9, sus2, add9, blues-inflected thirds, flat sevenths, octave bass figures, chromatic approach tones, and static harmonic beds that leave room for spectral movement instead of frequent chord changes.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Vowel Relay** — contrasting karinding mouth shapes trigger different ensemble responses and density states.
2. **Human Filter Hold** — obvious synth filter sweeps are suspended while the karinding is active so acoustic formant movement remains the only moving spectral filter.
3. **Jaw Gate** — selected electronic events are removed immediately after strong tongue releases to enlarge the acoustic pulse through negative space.
4. **Formant Chase** — tenor saxophone or clavinet answers the perceived overtone region rather than the jaw harp fundamental.
5. **Closed-Open Flip** — identical rhythmic figures repeat with opposite mouth-cavity shapes, changing spectral identity without changing the groove.
6. **Tongue Rest Bar** — one-bar karinding silence removes all acoustic formant motion before its return defines the next section.
7. **No-Jaw-Harp-Novelty Rule** — the karinding cannot be reduced to a comic boing, intro effect, or decorative loop; real mouth-shape variation must determine arrangement behavior.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through faster Vowel Relays, harder tongue releases, denser clavinet answers, stronger handclaps, higher saxophone Formant Chases, brighter mouth shapes, and shorter Jaw Gates. Climaxes remain dry, funky, and human-scaled rather than becoming festival-sized.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Formant Boogie formed in a former municipal language-laboratory building in Bandung that had been partially converted into rehearsal rooms. The facility retained rows of narrow listening booths, headphone jacks, speech-waveform charts, phonetics diagrams, smoked-glass windows, metal cassette drawers, green desk lamps, reel decks, and long acoustic curtains.

Karinding player **Raka Permana** had been using one booth because its dry walls made subtle mouth-cavity changes unusually audible. His background crossed Sundanese traditional performance, experimental ensemble work, and rhythm studies. He became interested in how listeners perceived large tonal changes even when the basic vibrating tongue of the instrument remained physically constant.

Producer **Nadine Yusuf** arrived with a compact drum machine, analog monosynth, clavinet, and a folder of unfinished boogie tracks. Her first arrangement tried to match Raka with resonant synth patches. The result obscured exactly what made his instrument interesting. She removed the synth filter movement, kept the bass timbre stable, and began noting mouth-shape changes on the arrangement sheet instead.

Bassist and synth programmer **Dimas Hartono** developed short analog-bass phrases that avoided the karinding's formant range. Keyboardist **Leila Grant** reduced her clavinet and electric-piano parts to narrow harmonic fragments. Tenor saxophonist **Bima Adikusuma** completed the group after learning to follow Raka's perceived overtone focus rather than simply doubling written notes.

Their first performances were called **vowel tests**. Behind Raka stood a small illuminated panel with five abstract mouth-shape diagrams. The images were not lyrics and did not trigger electronics. They simply showed the current resonant shape to the audience while the groove remained unchanged.

The visual language developed around cassette-beige, smoked-glass grey, phonetics-chart blue, bamboo brown, signal orange, black plastic, dull chrome, and fluorescent white. Wardrobe favors contemporary shirts, knit polos, loose trousers, simple dresses, sneakers, and work jackets rather than ceremonial costume or exaggerated retro-disco styling.

The unofficial rule is that if a producer can reproduce the track's central spectral motion by automating a low-pass filter after muting the karinding, the arrangement has failed.

