# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Same Reed, Different Mouth

**Story & Context:** At 114 BPM, Nadine opens with short drum-machine kick, crisp clap, closed sixteenth hats, and no harmonic instrument. Raka enters with a four-pulse karinding cell using one stable physical tongue rhythm. The first two cycles use a dark, narrow cavity shape. Dimas answers only with short tonic-octave bass. On the third cycle, Raka opens the mouth cavity dramatically without changing the pulse pattern. Leila responds with bright dominant-9 clavinet stabs and Bima answers the perceived upper formant with one tenor-sax sixth. The hook is a Closed-Open Flip: identical rhythm, identical drum pattern, different acoustic spectral identity. A one-bar Tongue Rest before the final chorus makes the return of the mouth-shaped resonance feel larger than any filter sweep.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old language-lab booth with two mouth-shape diagrams taped beside the same bamboo karinding, cassette deck paused at 00:14, orange indicator lamp reflected in smoked glass. Original sparse lyric fragments: “same reed / different mouth / nothing moved but the color / say it once more.”

## Track 2: Booth Seven Has No Filter Knob

**Story & Context:** At 118 BPM, this is the EP's firmest boogie track. Nadine uses four-on-the-floor kick only in the chorus; verses use displaced kick and tightly muted hats. Raka performs rapid Vowel Relays through three cavity shapes. Each state changes the arrangement: dark resonance leaves only bass, mid resonance adds clavinet, bright resonance adds clap reinforcement and short tenor-sax answers. Human Filter Hold is strict throughout the karinding sections, so the analog bass patch remains completely static. Midway through, Nadine mutes the karinding for eight bars and finally opens the synth filter slowly; when Raka returns, the synth snaps back to its stable tone and the acoustic mouth becomes the filter again.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Listening booth numbered 7, analog synth panel visible behind glass with its filter knob taped in place, karinding resting beneath a blue phonetics chart, one green desk lamp. Original sparse lyric fragments: “booth seven / leave the knob alone / I can shape it here / listen to the room in my mouth.”

## Track 3: Vowels After the Last Cassette

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 108 BPM and begins with clavinet, hand percussion, and sparse bass before the drums fully enter. Raka uses the slowest karinding pulses on the EP, holding each cavity shape long enough for its overtone field to become obvious. Bima's tenor saxophone performs Formant Chase in reverse: instead of following the brightest resonance upward, he descends into subtone whenever Raka opens the mouth cavity. Leila holds wide 6/9 electric-piano voicings during the chorus but drops them after every strong tongue release to create Jaw Gates. In the final minute, drums, keys, saxophone, and bass disappear one by one. Raka repeats one pulse while moving gradually from closed to open cavity shape without changing the fundamental source. The EP ends before the mouth fully closes again.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty language laboratory after midnight, last cassette ejected from a metal deck, five vowel diagrams reflected in smoked glass, one karinding on a desk beneath fluorescent light. Original sparse lyric fragments: “last cassette / vowels after midnight / one note wearing five faces / don't close the mouth yet.”

